in @prism
in @glass
in @meta
in @uuid/spectral
in @epistemologic/reality/time
in @kintsugi/roomba

# @mirror/store — the fragmentation store interface.
#
# Recognition (docs/specs/mirror-store.md, 2026-06-04 reframe):
# the substrate truth lives in the fragmentation store,
# content-addressed via OIDs. No external dependency resolution;
# no version conflict detection; no fetching. Same OID = same
# stored composition, always. Composition is by OID, not by name
# resolution.
#
# splinter_graph IS the structural lockfile. The OID-graph of
# children under a root splinter IS the dependency closure; the
# closure is the lockfile because the OIDs are the names. Nothing
# further pins what the OIDs already pin.
#
# Vocabulary alignment (2026-06-06 substrate-pull-realize):
# Per shards/glass.mirror's three-layer recognition, the universal
# atomic content-addressed unit at every altitude is `splinter`
# (declared at @glass; the BOTTOM layer). The (root, children)
# dependency-closure form was historically called `splinter` at
# this altitude — it has been renamed to `splinter_graph` to
# distinguish the atomic carrier (universal; one OID per atom)
# from the OID-graph projection (root + transitive closure of
# OIDs in the dependency tree). The substrate's stored composition
# unit (spectral_uuid-addressed) is `shard`, also declared at @glass.
#
# Alex's recognition (2026-06-06, post the spectral_uuid landing,
# realized 2026-06-06 second tick by Mara): `splinter_graph` IS
# `mosaic(@store)` — the (root, children) shape carries the same
# compositional meaning the universal mosaic algebra carries at
# every altitude. The parametric universal carrier
# `type mosaic(altitude) = ref` is declared at @mirror/mosaic
# (shards/mirror/mosaic.mirror); the @store-altitude specialization
# is `splinter_graph`'s concrete record form (substantive structure:
# root + children-closure). The two declarations agree on meaning;
# the concrete record shape stays where it is because the closure
# structure is load-bearing at this altitude, and a future tick
# may declare `type splinter_graph = mosaic(@store)` as a type
# alias once the substrate's altitude-parametric type aliasing
# matures (deferred substrate touch).
#
# The store IS canonical. `.shatter` files on disk are ONE
# OPTIONAL projection format of the store's content (per
# docs/specs/mosaic.md §7 and docs/specs/shatter-spec.md). Other
# projections are possible (binary, IR, language-specific
# embeddings); the store does not require any particular on-disk
# format. Whatever a tool projects FROM the store, the store's
# content addressing is the canonical identity. The store comes
# first; .shatter comes after.
#
# === Apache-2.0 rock-solid floor discipline ===
#
# Per collapse spec `docs/specs/mcp-spec-song-collapse.md` §11
# (Mara `2cfd2a7`, 2026-07-06 respawn): @mirror/store IS the minimum
# viable Apache-2.0 floor for content-addressed graph compilation.
# Recognition #43 ([[architecture-mirror-as-content-addressed-build-system]];
# mirror IS content-addressed build system; LANDED per collapse spec
# §11 promotion) grounds this shard as the substrate-decl of that
# recognition. The Apache-2.0 floor is legible at engineering
# altitude; the whole ecosystem depends on this being real and
# usable standalone — not a teaser, not a demo, not a consolation
# prize for adopters that skip @spectral/db.
#
# Per Alex's rock-solid-floor directive (spec §10.6, §11.6): the open
# floor MUST be useful in agentic workflows even without the
# @spectral/db magic. The nine §11.6 value-adds ARE first-order
# capabilities of the open floor; the closed engine sits ON TOP of
# them, not underneath them.
#
# === Non-negotiable v0 invariants ===
#
# The following are substrate invariants, not policies. They are
# declared at v0 to avoid the class of failure Nix's ca-derivations
# arc (2020-2023) documents: bolting content-addressing on later
# costs the ecosystem a decade of dual-mode operation. Per collapse
# spec §11.3.2, §11.3.3.
#
#   1. `oid` is immutable-under-hash. Once written, the bytes at an
#      oid cannot change. Ever. Not by mutation, not by version, not
#      by migration. `OID = BLAKE3(content)` by construction; the
#      content-address is a function of the bytes, so the identity is
#      immutable by construction. This is the immutability invariant.
#      Amendments happen by writing NEW bytes, obtaining a NEW oid,
#      and updating the reference (whether ref, splinter_graph child
#      link, or crystal head) to point at the new oid — the oid
#      itself never mutates.
#
#   2. Composition is purely-functional. Any `Prism<A, B>` acting on
#      stored objects is a total function `OID_A -> OID_B`;
#      deterministic; referentially transparent. Same input oid + same
#      prism composition + same store => byte-identical output oid,
#      verifiable by re-hash. This grounds the Dolstra 2006 lineage
#      (Nix's purely-functional deployment discipline) at the
#      substrate altitude. Consumers can rely on it; competing store
#      species MUST honor it.
#
# === Bazel REAPI floor decomposition ===
#
# Per collapse spec §11.3.6: the industrial minimum floor decomposes
# into two orthogonal open surfaces the Bazel Remote Execution API
# (REAPI) codified:
#
#   (1) OID -> object map — the Content-Addressable Storage (CAS)
#       surface. Byte-in / byte-out; hash-mediated identity. Realized
#       here as `read` / `write` / `exists` / `verify` on the six-op
#       surface below. This IS REAPI's `ContentAddressableStorage`
#       (`FindMissingBlobs` + `BatchReadBlobs` + `BatchUpdateBlobs`)
#       modulo naming.
#
#   (2) action-hash -> OID map — the action-cache surface. Given a
#       canonical action key (oid of the canonicalized action
#       description) return the previously-recorded output oids.
#       Forward-promised at M6 tick 3 as sub-prism
#       `@mirror/store/action_cache` with `record` and `lookup`
#       actions. This IS REAPI's `ActionCache`.
#
# Both surfaces are open at Apache-2.0. Consumers can use the CAS
# without the action-cache (naive byte-store); consumers wanting
# incremental rebuild use both. The split is orthogonal to the
# scheduler; per Mokhov/Mitchell/Peyton Jones "Build Systems à la
# Carte" (JFP 2020), the Rebuilder × Scheduler factoring is
# independent of the CAS layer @mirror/store declares — any scheduler
# discipline (topological / restarting / suspending) composes with
# this floor.
#
# === Prior-art anchors ===
#
# Cited to ground this shard against the actual state of the art;
# adopters recognize the shape:
#
#   - Dolstra 2006 PhD `The Purely Functional Software Deployment
#     Model` (TU Delft) — the canonical treatment of derivation-store
#     mathematics; Nix's foundational text. Every invariant above
#     traces to Dolstra's discipline (purely-functional composition,
#     immutable-by-hash storage, closure discipline for reachability
#     + GC, deterministic builds from typed inputs to content-
#     addressed outputs). The ca-derivations arc (2020-2023) grounds
#     the do-not-bolt-immutable-on-later warning.
#
#   - Mokhov, Mitchell, Peyton Jones `Build Systems à la Carte`
#     (JFP 2020) — the canonical taxonomy of build-system design.
#     The (scheduler: topological / restarting / suspending) ×
#     (rebuilder: dirty-bit / verifying-trace / constructive-trace /
#     deep-constructive-trace) grid. Nix = topological × verifying-
#     trace; Bazel = restarting × constructive-trace; Shake =
#     suspending. @mirror/store's Apache-2.0 floor is scheduler-
#     agnostic (any scheduler can drive it); the Rebuilder × Scheduler
#     factoring is orthogonal to the CAS-layer declaration this shard
#     makes. The closed engine @spectral/db adds a suspending
#     scheduler + spectral-embedding-based rebuilder — the fifth cell
#     the Mokhov et al. Table 1 leaves empty.
#
#   - IPFS MERKLE_DAG.md + Merkle 1979 (`Secrecy, Authentication,
#     and Public Key Systems`) — the content-addressing invariant
#     substrate. Merkle DAG discipline: content-addressed nodes; DAG
#     edges by content-address; deduplication by hash-collision;
#     verifiable partial pulls; federation-friendly by construction.
#     @mirror/store inherits via `splinter_graph`: the OID-graph IS a
#     Merkle DAG; any partial fetch is verifiable by re-hashing;
#     `verify(o, bytes) -> verdict` IS Merkle's original membership
#     check.
#
#   - Bazel REAPI (`github.com/bazelbuild/remote-apis`) — the
#     industry standard for the CAS + action-cache split named above.
#     The floor that every remote build system exposes as open
#     protocol; @mirror/store's six-op surface matches modulo naming.
#
# === Trichotomy at OID altitude (git-object-model analog) ===
#
# Per collapse spec §11.3.1 + [[architecture-splinter-and-spectral-db-edges]]:
# the substrate carries a three-layer content-addressed trichotomy
# analogous to git's blob/tree/commit but distinct because the
# substrate carries the SpectralUuid layer git lacks:
#
#   - `splinter` (leaf; @glass altitude) — the atomic content-
#     addressed unit. One splinter, one oid, one content. git's
#     `blob` analog.
#   - `splinter_graph` (composite; @mirror/store altitude, this
#     shard) — the (root, children) OID-graph projection; the
#     dependency-closure. git's `tree` analog. Substrate-canonical
#     name; DOES NOT collide with @mirror family-root (per
#     `feedback-substrate-already-had-the-word` 55+ instances,
#     Kagi-derived alternative names superseded).
#   - `crystal` (settled root; @mirror/store/crystal species
#     altitude) — the SpectralUuid-addressed settlement of a
#     composition. git's `commit` analog, extended with typed
#     context. LANDED at `shards/mirror/store/crystal.mirror`.
#
# === Agentic-workflow first-order capabilities (the open floor's offer) ===
#
# Per Alex's rock-solid-floor directive and collapse spec §11.6: the
# following are first-order substrate-decl'd capabilities of the open
# Apache-2.0 floor — NOT consolation prizes for adopters that skip
# @spectral/db, but the reason mirror is competitive at the open
# layer today. Every capability below discharges against the six-op
# surface + immutable-under-hash + purely-functional composition
# invariants above, with no @spectral/db magic required.
#
#   - **Deterministic compilation** — same inputs -> same oid,
#     always. Given the same input oid and the same prism
#     composition, the output oid is byte-identical across machines,
#     verifiable by hash. Makes agent-run divergence decidable at
#     the store layer: if input oids match and output oids differ,
#     it is a substrate bug.
#   - **Reproducible builds** — cross-machine reproducibility
#     (nix-copy analog). Agent A on machine X and agent B on machine
#     Y running the same composition against the same input oid
#     produce the same output oid. Auditors can re-run and check.
#   - **Session persistence** — MCP session state IS crystals in
#     the store. Agent restarts (crash, deploy, scale-down) do not
#     lose accumulated context; the next process reads the session
#     ref and resumes from the crystal. No in-process fragility;
#     no external SQL/Redis shim.
#   - **Cross-agent OID-addressed memory** — any two agents pointed
#     at the same @mirror/store instance share the same OID space.
#     Coordination reduces to hash-mediated pointer-passing (the oid
#     IS the coordination). No out-of-band message bus, no explicit
#     orchestrator required.
#   - **Provenance chains** — every artifact traces to its inputs
#     via closure; auditability IS a substrate primitive. Any
#     decision's causes ARE its input oids; any rollback IS a walk
#     backward through the OID-graph.
#   - **Verifiable computation** — any content-addressed DAG is
#     Merkle-verifiable. Trust boundaries reduce to hash boundaries;
#     adopters can prove what they built without trusting each other.
#
# These are the offer to adopters using ONLY @mirror/store. Most
# current agentic tooling does not deliver even these capabilities
# with closed backends. The open floor at Apache-2.0 makes them
# substrate primitives.
#
# === What @spectral/db adds (closed) — the deliberate fault plane ===
#
# Per collapse spec §11.4: @spectral/db (the closed engine) adds
# graph Laplacian eigenvalue navigation + sub-Turing Datalog-adjacent
# query surface + spectral-embedding-based structural proximity +
# cellular sheaf primitives (per Recognition #34 eigenboard-is-sheaf).
# These are OPTIONAL on top of the open floor; @mirror/store MUST
# remain usable standalone without @spectral/db.
#
# The fault plane is deliberate: storage protocol open (Apache-2.0),
# query / scheduler / navigation closed (proprietary). This is the
# same fault plane git/GitHub, Nix/Cachix, IPFS/Filecoin, and Bazel/
# BuildBuddy discovered independently — five ecosystems drew the
# line at the same place; @mirror/store inherits.
#
# === Typed edges declared at @edge (fault-plane collapse) ===
#
# Per Alex adjudication 2026-07-08 "collapse into one surface" for the
# @edge fault-plane: typed edges are declared at `shards/edge.mirror`
# (@edge; LANDED 2026-07-08) as substrate-decl of edges as @magic
# instances. The prior deferral text ("typed edges are reserved for
# @spectral/db") was structurally incoherent: `shards/mirror/ref.mirror`
# had already declared `edge_kind` as a seven-variant substrate
# discriminant at navigation altitude, and the store's `walk` /
# `impacted_by` closure algebra was already consuming edge instances
# implicitly via `splinter_graph`. The collapse names the shape
# honestly at family-root altitude.
#
# What @spectral/db specializes on TOP of the shared @edge surface:
# the NUMERICAL DIMENSIONS of `edge_weight` (Laplacian matrix entries,
# eigenvalue-navigation coefficients, spectral-embedding coordinates)
# per the @magic/mechanism mapping at @edge — the weight field is
# matter-hidden at @edge altitude; @spectral/db is the closed
# specialization that opens the mechanism at its private altitude.
# The open floor gets the typed carrier + kind discriminant + walk
# closure algebra; the closed engine gets the numerical spectral
# navigation on top of the shared substrate.
#
# `walk(root: oid) -> splinter_graph` answers reachability + ancestry
# + closure at the OID-graph altitude — Nix's `-q` discipline exactly.
# It walks @edge instances implicitly (the (root, children)
# projection); it does not answer *which OIDs are STRUCTURALLY NEAR
# this OID in the spectral embedding* — that requires the numerical
# specialization of edge_weight that lives in @spectral/db.
#
# === Reverse-closure at family-root: walk + impacted_by ===
#
# Forward closure alone is half the reachability algebra. `walk(root:
# oid) -> splinter_graph` answers "what does this OID reach?" — the
# forward transitive closure of children under a root. The reverse
# question — "what reaches this OID?" — is equally load-bearing at
# build-system altitude and has an equally-canonical prior-art shape:
# Bazel's `bazel query 'rdeps(//..., //target:foo)'` — the reverse-
# dependency query — is the industry-standard vocabulary. This shard
# declares that surface at family-root as `impacted_by(oid: oid) ->
# [oid]`, the reverse-closure complement of `walk`. Together the two
# actions close the reachability algebra of the OID-graph at family-
# root altitude; every downstream question (invalidation, provenance,
# blast radius, dependency inversion) decomposes into one of the two.
#
# The pair also names the substrate's reverse-dependency query in
# vocabulary older than Bazel: Karen Spärck Jones (1972, `A Statistical
# Interpretation of Term Specificity and its Application in Retrieval`)
# grounds reverse-lookup as the second half of any content-addressed
# retrieval discipline — the forward index answers "what does this
# document contain?"; the reverse (inverted) index answers "what
# documents contain this term?". `impacted_by` IS the OID-graph
# analog: given an OID, name the OIDs whose forward closure contains
# it. Reverse-frequency + content-address + typed reverse-lookup is
# the whole discipline; the substrate carries it end-to-end.
#
# === N-cascade composition — impacted_by is the invalidation key ===
#
# Per the N-cascade landed 2026-07-05 through 2026-07-06:
#
#   - N1 (`2857fb1`): `@epistemologic/property/verdict_is_content_addressed`
#     — the predicate that memoization of verdicts is valid by
#     construction. The property discharges once inputs are content-
#     addressed and composition is purely-functional (both invariants
#     of this shard, above).
#   - N2 (`0a72c42`): `@mirror/store/action_cache` species — the
#     concrete cache surface with `cache_read` / `cache_write` /
#     `cache_exists` at the three-OID key (action-oid, input-oid,
#     context-oid). This IS REAPI's `ActionCache` in the floor
#     decomposition above.
#   - N3 (`756f2f7`): the Rust wiring in `cmd_kintsugi_spec` at
#     `bootstrap/src/lib.rs` — `cache_read` before cargo, `cache_write`
#     after. Consumer chain at SEVEN per Seam audit `09b850c`.
#   - **N4 (this shard's enrichment)**: `impacted_by(oid: oid) ->
#     [oid]` at family-root — the REVERSE closure that makes the
#     action_cache surgically invalidatable. On a git diff of any
#     touched OID, `impacted_by` names the downstream OIDs whose
#     cached action-verdicts at N2 `@mirror/store/action_cache` may
#     need to be re-verdicted; only those entries invalidate, not the
#     whole cache. Without `impacted_by`, invalidation degenerates to
#     "flush everything on any diff" (the Nix ca-derivations pre-2020
#     failure mode); with it, invalidation is surgical.
#   - N5 (forward-promised): `@kintsugi/store/git` commit-as-fold —
#     the composition that folds a batch of writes into a single
#     content-addressed settlement.
#
# The composition N4 -> N2 is the substrate's operational witness
# for candidate recognition `cross-species-discharge-is-first-class`
# (needs 2 more witnesses per Seam registry): `impacted_by` at
# @mirror/store family-root discharges the N2 action_cache species'
# invalidation contract without either action naming the other in
# body — the composition is first-class at the substrate-decl surface
# where both agree on `oid`. `verdict_is_content_addressed` (N1)
# grounds the composition semantically; N3 wires it operationally;
# N4 makes the wiring surgical.
#
# Recognition #43 ([[architecture-mirror-as-content-addressed-build-system]];
# mirror IS content-addressed build system) grounds the whole cascade:
# `impacted_by` is the substrate's declared surface for the same
# reverse-dependency query Bazel/Buck2/Nix/Shake all expose at their
# respective build-system altitudes. The substrate already had the
# word (Bazel `rdeps`); this tick names it in-substrate.
#
# === Projection API (oid <-> working-tree) ===
#
# Per collapse spec §11.3.5: the store is the truth; the disk is a
# projection. The Apache-2.0 floor formalizes the projection API as
# operational escape hatch to the filesystem (git-object-model
# analog):
#
#   - `project(o: oid, path) -> imperfect` — write the oid's content
#     to the filesystem path (analog: `git checkout` at blob
#     altitude). Renders the store's truth onto the working-tree.
#   - `unproject(path) -> imperfect(oid)` — hash the file at path,
#     return its oid (analog: `git hash-object`). Used for `mirror
#     status`-style diffs against the working-tree.
#
# Forward-promised at M6 tick 2 as sub-prism
# `@mirror/store/projection`. Without these two ops, the DAG-as-
# source-of-truth discipline in collapse spec §3.6 has no
# operational escape hatch to disk. With them, adopters build the
# full round-trip: edit shard on disk -> unproject to new oid ->
# write to store -> update ref; or read head ref -> project each oid
# to canonical disk path -> working-tree renders store's truth.
#
# Substrate placement: per
# [[architecture-fragmentation-is-the-rust-substrate]] the Rust
# implementation lives in `fragmentation/src/*.rs` (Fragmentable,
# Fractal, ConcurrentStore, HashAlg, Cid). This shard declares the
# substrate-level surface those impls realize.
#
# Spec context: docs/specs/mirror-store.md §§3, 4, 5, 7
# (surface, audit, boot, peer-to-spectral-db).
# Substrate decisions: [[architecture-shards-as-substrate-source]],
#                      [[architecture-prism-as-trait-as-everything]],
#                      [[architecture-fragmentation-is-the-rust-substrate]],
#                      [[architecture-glass-wall-substrate-types]].
# Related shards: glass.mirror (verdict / imperfect / transparency),
#                 cli.mirror (uses `ref(@mirror/store/oid)`),
#                 mosaic.mirror (the build that settles ON this store).
# Path-namespace property: this file at `shards/mirror/store.mirror`
# declares `@mirror/store` and only that.

# === oid — the content-addressed identity primitive ===
#
# A sub-prism scoped to @mirror/store. `oid` IS the substrate's
# identity carrier: a content address produced by the hash algebra
# (today CoincidenceHash<5,5>, per shards/prism.mirror's spectral-
# triple framing). Per the cli.mirror precedent — `type
# content_address = ref(@mirror/store/oid)` — the oid is named at
# this sub-prism path so call sites in other shards resolve it
# unambiguously.
#
# `oid` is NOT a primitive scalar; it's a typed reference whose
# content IS its identity. Construction is by hashing bytes;
# equality is byte-equality of the resulting reference.
glass @mirror/store/oid {
  focus oid
  project oid
  split oid
  shift oid
  settle oid
}

# The oid type. `ref` is the substrate's content-reference carrier
# (per boot/std/git.mirror's `type oid(ref)` precedent and
# shards/glass.mirror's `file: ref` location form).
type oid = ref

# === splinter_graph — the OID-graph; the structural lockfile ===
#
# splinter_graph is the graph of (oid -> [oid]) — a root splinter's
# content address plus the content addresses of its children.
# Composition is by OID: a splinter references its dependencies by
# their oids directly, not by name. The closure of children under
# a root IS the dependency closure; that closure IS the lockfile.
# There is no external file to pin versions; the OID-graph already
# pins them by construction.
#
# Distinct from `splinter` declared at @glass (the universal
# content-addressed atom). The atom carries one (content, altitude,
# transparency) triple; the graph carries a root + the transitive
# closure of its children's OIDs. The atom is the unit; the graph
# is the dependency-closure projection of a composition of atoms
# at the @store altitude.
#
# A `shard` (per @glass) is the SpectralUuid-addressed settlement of
# a composition; its splinter set is the input to the composition;
# the splinter_graph below is the OID-graph view of that closure as
# seen from @store's wire surface. The three altitudes:
#
#   @glass: splinter (atom), shard (settlement)
#   @store: splinter_graph (OID-graph projection of the closure),
#           shard_ref (typed handle to a stored shard)
#
# Per docs/specs/mirror-store.md §1.2: "splinter_graph IS the
# structural lockfile. Composition by OID, not name resolution."
type splinter_graph = {
  root: oid,
  children: [oid],
}

# === bytes — the raw payload type ===
#
# Substrate scalar. Used for read/write payloads at the store's
# wire surface. Per cli.mirror's `type str = ref(scalar/string)`
# precedent, declared here as a store-altitude reference so the
# read/write actions have a typed body.
type bytes = ref(scalar/bytes)

# === shard_ref — the typed handle to a stored shard ===
#
# A shard reference resolves to its stored shard at the bound
# identifier. Distinct from `oid` itself: a shard_ref carries the
# expectation that the identifier resolves to a `shard` (per @glass:
# a uuid_spectral-addressed settled composition of splinters), not
# an arbitrary blob.
#
# Substrate-pull-realize (2026-06-06 second tick, Mara): the
# identifier carrier is `uuid_spectral` (declared at
# shards/uuid/spectral.mirror as @uuid/spectral — a sub-prism under
# @uuid, the universal 128-bit identifier root). The lift completes
# the three-layer recognition — shard.id IS uuid_spectral by
# construction (per shards/glass.mirror's shard declaration);
# shard_ref names a uuid_spectral that resolves to a shard. The
# deferred lift in mosaic-as-type-system.md §9.7 closes here.
#
# Realised in Rust as `fragmentation/src/shard_ref.rs`'s
# `ShardRef { id: SpectralUuid, context: ShardContext,
# budget_bytes: BudgetBytes }`. The substrate names what shard_ref
# IS; the Rust crate names how the typed handle is implemented.
#
# Fetch failures emit `imperfect` (the @glass loss carrier).
type shard_ref = uuid_spectral

# === the store's surface — six operations ===
#
# These are the @io contract the bootstrap's MirrorStore trait
# (per docs/specs/mirror-store.md §3) realizes. They are also the
# contract `mirror shatter` (plumbing CLI, per mosaic.md §7 and
# spec §6) invokes when it cats content out of the store. No
# porcelain here; the porcelain is in @mirror/mosaic and
# @mirror/kintsugi.

# read(oid) — fetch the content at an oid. `imperfect` carries
# the bytes on success, opacity on failure (oid not present, oid
# present but content drifted past verify, oid present but kind
# mismatch). Per glass.mirror's `imperfect(a, e, l)` floor.
read(o: oid) -> imperfect { \ }

# write(bytes) — insert content; return the oid the substrate
# computed for it. Idempotent by construction: writing the same
# bytes twice returns the same oid and is a no-op on the second
# call (the content-address is a function of the bytes).
write(content: bytes) -> oid { \ }

# exists(oid) — verdict on whether the oid resolves in this
# store. `verdict` from @glass: pass | partial(confidence) |
# failure(reason). `partial` is the case where the oid is in a
# cache layer but the canonical backend has not confirmed
# (rare; documented for completeness).
exists(o: oid) -> verdict { \ }

# diff(a, b) — the content-addressed delta between two oids.
# `imperfect` because the diff carries opacity: when a and b
# share no common ancestor in their splinter_graph closures, the
# diff is a full replacement (partial(opacity_map) reporting the
# size and shape of the replacement). When they're identical,
# success(empty). When one isn't in the store, failure(reason).
diff(a: oid, b: oid) -> imperfect { \ }

# walk(oid) — enumerate the splinter_graph closure rooted at oid.
# Returns the OID-graph as a flat list of (parent, child)
# edges; the caller reconstructs the tree. The closure IS the
# lockfile; walk IS the resolution.
walk(root: oid) -> splinter_graph { \ }

# impacted_by(oid) — the REVERSE closure complement of walk. Given
# an oid, enumerate the OIDs whose forward `walk` closure contains
# it — the substrate's declared reverse-dependency query surface
# (Bazel `rdeps` analog at store altitude, Karen Spärck Jones 1972
# inverted-index discipline at the OID-graph altitude). Returns a
# flat `[oid]`; the caller composes further (e.g. against N2
# `@mirror/store/action_cache` `cache_exists` for surgical
# invalidation of cached verdicts whose input closure crossed the
# argument oid). `walk` + `impacted_by` together close the OID-graph
# reachability algebra at family-root: forward closure ("what does
# this OID reach?") + reverse closure ("what reaches this OID?").
# Realisation lives in fragmentation (per
# [[architecture-fragmentation-is-the-rust-substrate]]); family-root
# declares the contract, obligation body discharges at @io.
impacted_by(oid: oid) -> [oid] { \ }

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# === query(store, predicate) — the filter-fold action ===
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# Mint per Mara 2026-07-17 (`[substrate-pull:realize]` composition-only
# mint; ZERO Rust). Consumer-pulled by `@silicon/algebra.tray_content_
# source` (`shards/silicon/algebra.mirror` :164-202 pending flag) which
# reads @mirror/store's mirror-authored bilateral-retirement crystals
# as the seed of `@silicon/algebra`'s tray under the composition graph
# at :113-141.
#
# Alex 2026-07-17 in-transcript verbatim (session-crystallizing):
#
#   "the source of truth for content-addressed storage is
#    @mirror/store."
#
# THIS action IS the substrate-honest realisation of that authority at
# the query surface. No `@io/git.log` bypass; no side-channel enumeration;
# the store IS the query surface.
#
# === Substrate-honest composition over LANDED primitives ===
#
# query is NOT a new @io primitive. It IS a shard-body composition over
# THREE already-landed substrate primitives:
#
#   1. walk(root: oid) -> splinter_graph           :532-536 above
#        enumerate the OID-graph closure reachable from root
#   2. read(o: oid) -> imperfect                   :505-509 above
#        fetch content bytes at an OID
#   3. discharge(decl: bilateral, args: [ref]) -> verdict
#        `shards/epistemologic/pact/bilateral.mirror` :271 (Mara
#        `a0f4d3f` 2026-07-16); the reflective evaluator's byte-level
#        sentinel-check on the predicate declaration + args
#
# The fold: for each OID reachable via `walk(store_root_of(store))`,
# `read(oid)` to obtain content, resolve `predicate` (a ref) to its
# `bilateral` declaration in the corpus, `discharge(decl, [content_
# ref])`; collect OIDs where verdict is Pass. Return the filtered
# `[query_result]` sequence.
#
# ZERO new Rust needed at query-action altitude. The reflective
# evaluator (`bootstrap/src/apply_h.rs` per Reed `21fc211` Landing 3+4)
# already dispatches `walk` / `read` / `discharge` via @io primitives;
# the fold-body composes them per the shard-body discipline (per
# `shards/glue/fold_back.mirror` fold-over-tray precedent).
#
# === Consumer chain (composition graph forward-promises) ===
#
# 1. `@silicon/algebra.tray_content_source` (`shards/silicon/algebra.
#    mirror` :164-202) — reads mirror-authored bilateral-arm retirement
#    crystals as `@silicon/algebra`'s tray seed. Filter predicate is
#    `@bilateral(@facet/rust, @facet/mirror).translation_admissible`
#    (per `shards/epistemologic/pact/bilateral.mirror` :677-681, the
#    first general-case `@bilateral(A, B)` instance).
#
# 2. `@fate.roll` reads `@silicon/algebra`'s tray via this action per
#    the composition graph at `shards/silicon/algebra.mirror` :246-266
#    (write-back protocol). Each `translate_rust_to_mirror` discharge
#    (`shards/kintsugi/translate.mirror::translate_rust_to_mirror` at
#    Mara `86dec5e`) writes a NEW mirror-authored crystal at Edge 6;
#    next `query` invocation returns the enlarged tray (bauchladen-
#    monotonic per `shards/bauchladen.mirror` §"crystal accumulation").
#
# 3. `@fate.tournament` reads `@fate/algebra`'s path-namespace via
#    this action (composition-graph forward-promise; the tournament
#    dispatcher enumerates ranked outcomes as filtered @mirror/store
#    query results).
#
# === Audit chain ===
#
# Substrate-already-had-the-word citation chain THIS mint composes over:
#
#   - `2675d3e` (Mara THIS session; `shards/silicon/algebra.mirror` tray
#      source pending flag) — the consumer that surfaced the missing
#      query surface + the composition graph the fold discharges
#   - `a0f4d3f` (Mara 2026-07-16; `shards/epistemologic/pact/bilateral.
#      mirror` grammar-decl'd shape) — the `bilateral` typed carrier
#      + `discharge` action THIS query composes the predicate side over
#   - `86dec5e` (Mara THIS session; `shards/kintsugi/translate.mirror`
#      translate composition) — the write-back producer whose Edge-6
#      crystals THIS query reads back
#   - Alex 2026-07-17 verbatim: "the source of truth for content-
#      addressed storage is @mirror/store" — the substrate authority
#      landing THIS query at @mirror/store, not @io/git.log
#
# === Substrate-decl'd query_result carrier ===
#
# The per-element result carrier. Each surviving OID + the discharge
# verdict that certified its survival (Pass by construction; Fail-
# verdicts filter out during the fold and never appear in the output
# sequence, but the field is retained so downstream consumers may
# introspect the verdict shape for provenance).
#
# Identity contract: byte-equality on (oid, verdict). Two query_results
# are equal iff their oid and verdict fields both match. The realisation
# layer enforces.
type query_result = {
  oid:     oid,      # the crystal OID satisfying the predicate
  verdict: verdict,  # the discharge verdict (Pass by construction; Fail-
                     # verdicts are filtered out during the fold and do
                     # not appear in the output sequence)
}

# === query_composition_admissible — bilateral predicate (arity 1) ===
#
# Bilateral predicate discharging that a `query` invocation's action
# ref is a well-formed composition over LANDED walk + read + discharge
# per the docblock composition graph above. Pass iff the action
# reference resolves to a `query`-shaped composition whose fold-body
# names (A) an `@mirror/store.walk` enumeration edge + (B) an
# `@mirror/store.read` per-element fetch edge + (C) an
# `@epistemologic/pact/bilateral.discharge` per-element predicate
# discharge edge + (D) a filter-projection edge onto Pass-verdict
# elements. Discharge is byte-check on the composition graph's sentinel
# per THIS shard's docblock; NOT program-semantics inspection.
#
# Sentinel per shard docblock: `query=walks-reads-discharges-composed`.
#
# Dogfoods the grammar-decl'd bilateral shape from `a0f4d3f`. Arity 1
# (checks one composition-source ref per invocation). Body `\`-
# obligation-blocked; the reflective evaluator (per `21fc211` Landing
# 3+4) discharges via corpus lookup + sentinel byte-check per the same
# discipline `silicon_tray_content_addressed` (`shards/silicon/algebra.
# mirror` :337-379) uses.
#
# Rice-safe: pure sentinel-byte-check on a composition graph ref; no
# program-semantics inspection.
#
# Sibling to:
#   - `silicon_tray_content_addressed(source)` at `shards/silicon/
#     algebra.mirror` :337-379 — the consumer bilateral that certifies
#     the tray_content_source composition graph. THIS predicate
#     certifies the query action THAT composition composes over.
#   - `gc_reachability_closure_second_witness(refs, dangling)` at
#     :739-743 above — the arity-2 sibling on THIS same family root.
bilateral query_composition_admissible {
  sentinel "query=walks-reads-discharges-composed"
  arity    1
}
query_composition_admissible(source: ref) -> verdict { \ }

# === query — the filter-fold action ===
#
# THE LOAD-BEARING filter-fold. Given an @mirror/store `store_ref` +
# a bilateral predicate ref, folds `walk(store_root) → read → discharge`
# over the OID-graph closure; returns the filtered `[query_result]`
# sequence of OIDs whose bytes discharge `predicate` Pass.
#
# store:      family-root-typed handle to an open @mirror/store instance
#              (species-altitude wire handles like `@mirror/store/git`'s
#              `git_store` specialize at their altitude and discharge
#              through their per-species walk + read surface).
# predicate:  ref resolving to a `bilateral` declaration in the corpus
#              (per `shards/epistemologic/pact/bilateral.mirror` :229-234
#              typed carrier). The reflective evaluator resolves the ref
#              at dispatch time and passes the declaration to `discharge`.
#
# Returns: `[query_result]` — sequence of surviving OIDs each paired
# with their Pass-verdict. Order follows walk-traversal order (per the
# underlying `walk` action's OID-graph enumeration discipline);
# consumers requiring canonical-sort project further.
#
# The `requires query_composition_admissible(self)` clause names the
# discharge condition per recognition #37 (Pask-agreement reading of
# `requires`): the action commits to producing results whose composition
# graph satisfies the walk+read+discharge shape; consumers surface
# violations at the species boundary.
#
# Identity contract for the action's outcome: byte-equality on
# (store.oid, predicate.oid). Two invocations against a byte-equal
# @mirror/store state with a byte-equal predicate ref produce byte-equal
# `[query_result]` sequences under content-addressing. Monotonic growth
# (per @mirror/store §"Non-negotiable v0 invariants" immutability-under-
# hash + bauchladen crystal accumulation): future invocations produce
# SUPERSETS as new crystals accumulate; past matches persist.
#
# Body `\`-obligation-blocked per [[feedback-craft-not-deliver]]; the
# reflective evaluator composes the fold over LANDED walk + read +
# discharge primitives — ZERO Rust extension at query-action altitude.
#
# Realisation dispatch:
#   walk_result   = walk(store_root_of(store))       // :532 above
#   for each oid in walk_result.children:
#     content_ref = read(oid)                        // :509 above
#     verdict     = discharge(predicate_decl,        // bilateral.mirror :271
#                             [content_ref])
#     if verdict is Pass:
#       yield query_result { oid, verdict }
query(store: store_ref, predicate: ref) -> [query_result]
requires query_composition_admissible(source)
{ \ }

# verify(oid, bytes) — re-hash the bytes; verdict on whether
# the result equals the claimed oid. The substrate's integrity
# check; the Merkle property per
# [[architecture-fragmentation-is-the-rust-substrate]].
verify(o: oid, content: bytes) -> verdict { \ }

# === Species at @mirror/store ===
#
# @mirror/store is open at the species altitude — concrete backends
# specialize the six-op surface (read/write/exists/diff/walk/verify)
# with their own wire vocabulary. Each species shard declares HOW its
# wire verbs DISCHARGE to the canonical six-op surface inherited from
# @mirror/store.
#
# Landed:
#   @mirror/store/git    — git-backed namespaced wire (fragmentation/vcs/git)
#
# Forward-promised (consumer-pull):
#   @mirror/store/mem    — in-memory wire (testing + ephemeral)
#   @mirror/store/s3     — object-store wire (cloud)
#   @mirror/store/oci    — OCI artifact wire (registry-backed)
#   @mirror/store/<...>  — other species when consumers pull
#
# Per Alex 2026-06-30 substrate-pull: admitted the wire altitude as
# REAL (path α from Reed's drift observation at 60f5b7d). The wire is
# now a declared altitude of the substrate, not implementation detail
# routed around the substrate-decl surface.

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# 2026-07-16 ADDITIVE CASCADE — dangling-fragment two-phase gc surface
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# Companion cascade to `shards/kintsugi/roomba.mirror` (same tick)
# discharging Alex 2026-07-16 in-transcript crystallization: the
# @roomba's `vacuum` action marks a `fragment` for garbage collection
# per git-scm two-phase discipline. This @mirror/store cascade mints
# the family-root gc primitives vacuum composes over.
#
# Canonical spec: `docs/specs/roomba-bump-and-vacuum-as-first-order-
# autopoietic-motions.md` §5 (Mara `d457501`; the three gc primitives
# named + git-scm two-phase precedent + IPFS pin-as-reachability
# alternate discipline).
#
# Math foundation: `docs/math/kintsugi/roomba/bump-and-vacuum.md` §2-3
# (Mara `17697e6`; walk_dangling O(|V|+|E|) + mark_unreachable
# idempotency + prune safety invariant + Shannon-entropy grounding
# for two-phase gc as bounded-entropy operation).
#
# === Substrate-already-had-the-word ===
#
# `walk_dangling` is the git-fsck --dangling surface at family-root
# altitude; `mark_unreachable` is the mark-phase per canonical mark-
# and-sweep (McCarthy 1960); `prune` is git-gc's --prune=<duration>
# semantics verbatim. Delightfully-boring per AGENTS.md §Delightfully
# Boring: every reader who has run `git gc` already knows what these
# actions do.
#
# === Composition with existing walk / impacted_by ===
#
# The two closures LANDED at family-root close the reachability algebra:
#   walk(root) -> splinter_graph        forward closure (LANDED)
#   impacted_by(oid) -> [oid]           reverse closure (LANDED, N4)
#
# The three new primitives BELOW extend the algebra with the
# dangling/mark/prune discipline:
#   walk_dangling(refs) -> [fragment]    live-root-set complement enumeration
#   mark_unreachable(fragment) -> imperfect<gc_mark>   two-phase gc mark phase
#   prune(store, before) -> verdict                    two-phase gc delete phase
#
# A fragment is safely vacuum-able iff `impacted_by(f.oid) ∩ L(R) = ∅`
# where L(R) = ⋃_{r ∈ refs} walk(r). Per math §2.5 dangling-consistency
# proposition: this is equivalent (in the store's DAG) to `f.oid ∉ L(R)`
# — the reverse-check IS the second-witness of dangling-status.

# === walk_dangling — enumerate dangling fragments ===
#
# Enumerate the OIDs in the store whose forward reachability from ANY
# live ref is zero. The complement of the union of walk(ref) closures
# over all named refs. Realizes the git-fsck "dangling objects" surface
# at family-root altitude.
#
# refs: the set of live named-ref oids the store considers roots.
#        Empty list means "no live roots" — every OID is dangling.
#
# Returns: list of fragment records, each carrying the dangling OID +
# the tick the walker observed it dangling + the walker position at
# discovery.
#
# Realisation lives in fragmentation (per
# [[architecture-fragmentation-is-the-rust-substrate]]); family-root
# declares the contract; obligation body discharges at @io.
#
# Complexity per math §2.3: O(|V| + |E|) on the reachable subgraph,
# plus O(|V|) for the set-difference. Dominant cost is the enumeration
# of V \ reachable for the fragment list construction.
#
# Body `\`-obligation-blocked per [[feedback-craft-not-deliver]].
# === store_ref — the family-root open-store handle ===
#
# Substrate-family-root-honest typed handle naming one open @mirror/store
# instance. Species-altitude wire handles (e.g., @mirror/store/git's
# git_store) specialize this carrier at their altitude; the family-root
# actions (walk_dangling / mark_unreachable / prune) take store_ref so
# any @mirror/store species can dispatch through the same surface.
#
# Realisation lives in fragmentation; family-root declares the typed
# handle; species-altitude realises per its wire discipline.
type store_ref = ref

walk_dangling(refs: [oid]) -> [fragment] { \ }

# === mark_unreachable — record dangling observation ===
#
# Record the fragment's dangling observation in the store's gc metadata.
# Idempotent on fragment.oid: re-marking the same OID within a single
# prune-horizon window is a no-op on the mark-age (preserves the
# earliest observation per math §3.1 idempotency clause).
#
# The gc_mark returned carries:
#   marked_at        — canonical tick at store-recording time
#   prune_horizon    — marked_at + configured grace-period
#
# The grace-period is configured per-store at open-time; the substrate
# names the shape at family-root altitude. Per canonical spec §5.3:
# default 2 weeks (git-gc precedent); configurable to zero (IPFS-style
# aggressive collection) or infinity (pin-forever).
#
# Body `\`-obligation-blocked.
mark_unreachable(fragment: fragment) -> imperfect<gc_mark, ref, transparency(ref)> { \ }

# === prune — two-phase gc delete phase ===
#
# Delete every fragment whose gc_mark.prune_horizon is strictly less
# than `before`. Two-phase discipline: this is the DELETE phase; marks
# are the MARK phase. Realizes git-gc's `--prune=<duration>` semantics.
#
# before: cutoff tick. Fragments whose gc_mark.prune_horizon < before
#          are pruned; fragments whose prune_horizon >= before survive.
#
# Safety invariant per math §3.2: no fragment is deleted whose
# `impacted_by(oid) ∩ L(R) ≠ ∅` at prune-time (the mark phase
# established dangling-status; the grace-period bounds the concurrent-
# writer safety window per git-gc(1) rationale).
#
# @io discharge point (the single linearization crossing this cascade
# adds; pipeforward §5.5.4 hard-gate satisfied by construction: bump +
# vacuum + mark_unreachable + walk_dangling stay nonlinear; only prune
# crosses).
#
# L(ϕ) profile per pipeforward §5.5.4 rule 3:
# - Lossless for all fragments whose gc_mark.prune_horizon >= before
#   (unchanged bytes on disk).
# - Lossy by construction for all fragments whose gc_mark.prune_horizon
#   < before (content deleted from local store; may be recoverable from
#   remotes if @spectral/db replication enabled; forward-promised
#   recoverable via cold-storage tier per canonical spec §3.4).
#
# Body `\`-obligation-blocked; discharge is @io/fs delete on the store's
# on-disk objects directory (`.git/<namespace>/objects/` per
# @mirror/store/git wire species).
#
# store: a family-root-typed handle to an open @mirror/store instance;
#         species-altitude wire handles (e.g., @mirror/store/git's
#         git_store) specialize this carrier at their altitude and
#         discharge prune through their per-species delete surface.
prune(store: store_ref, before: tick) -> verdict { \ }

# === gc_reachability_closure_second_witness — bilateral predicate ===
#
# Does the dangling-set the walker enumerated match the reverse-closure
# verdict? Per math §2.5 dangling-consistency: for any o in the returned
# [fragment] list, `impacted_by(o.oid) ∩ L(R) = ∅` MUST hold. The
# forward-walk enumeration + reverse-closure check are two witnesses
# of the same property; discharge is the intersection-empty verdict
# at every returned fragment.
#
# Rice-safe bound: reads only byte-visible state (fragment.oid membership
# check against impacted_by output; set-intersection cardinality).
#
# Sentinel per shard docblock: `gc=reachability-second-witness-holds`
# — the byte-level substrate-decl'd marker. Discharge is byte-equality
# on the composed sentinel across all returned fragments.
#
# Body `\`-obligation-blocked. Consumers cite in `requires` clauses when
# invoking walk_dangling to certify the enumeration is dangling-consistent.
bilateral gc_reachability_closure_second_witness {
  sentinel "gc=reachability-second-witness-holds"
  arity 2
}
gc_reachability_closure_second_witness(refs: [oid], dangling: [fragment]) -> verdict { \ }

out @mirror/store
out @mirror/store/oid
out oid
out splinter_graph
out bytes
out shard_ref
out read
out write
out exists
out diff
out walk
out impacted_by
out query_result
out query_composition_admissible
out query
out verify
out store_ref
out walk_dangling
out mark_unreachable
out prune
out gc_reachability_closure_second_witness
